1A = relu(zA)\n- zB = x·W1B + bB\n\nSo B's units are approximately linear combinations of A's units, to the extent that A's units span the ink patterns that B responds to. Both nets also learned exactly zero weights on the same constant-background pixels (0, 24, 32, 39), which confirms they see the same input statistics.\n\n**Reducing the image to regions.**\n- I split the image into 2×2 blocks.\n- I used the 8 inner blocks (columns 2–5), where most digit ink and variance sit. I dropped the low-ink border columns from the mapping.\n- For each A unit and each B unit, I summed its four weights inside each block. This gives an 8-vector per unit: its response to ink in that block.\n\n**Building M.** I treated h1A ≈ zA and fitted a linear regression from A's units to B's pre-activations.\n- I approximated the input covariance as isotropic over blocks.\n- I approximated Cov(hA) by its diagonal, i.e. a frame or transpose decoder.\n- This gives M[j,k] = ½ · (aⱼ·bₖ) / (aⱼ·aⱼ).\n- The ½ corrects for redundancy: 16 A-units span only an 8-dimensional block space, so a plain projection sum would over-count by about 2.\n- I halved the row for A unit 3 again. It is weak and almost always negative, so it is usually off after the relu.\n\n**Building c.** I chose c so the mean is preserved: c = E[zB] − E[h1A]·M.\n- I estimated E[zB] from B's bias plus the typical mean digit image, including the border columns.\n- I estimated E[h1A] as E[relu(zA)] under a rough Gaussian spread.\n\nThis is a hand approximation, done with weights rounded to one decimal and guessed image statistics. I have not tested it. I expect it to beat chance clearly but stay well below B's own 95–97%.\n\n**Bridge:**\n```json\n{\"M\": [\n[-0.094,-0.277,0.028,0.179,-0.055,-0.148,0.087,0.247,0.085,-0.077,-0.148,0.122,-0.230,0.254,0.014,0.002,0.307,-0.084,-0.068,0.234,-0.069,0.006,-0.089,0.020],\n[0.115,0.002,0.010,-0.008,0.193,0.202,-0.014,-0.221,-0.169,-0.430,-0.054,0.158,-0.104,0.114,-0.213,-0.071,-0.178,-0.235,-0.0